    <h1>About Me</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      I am an independent AI and machine-learning consultant based in Paris, working
      remotely with clients across Europe. I build AI systems for production — RAG, agents
      and LLM pipelines — and forecasting models where a decision depends on the number. I
      hold a PhD in computer science from <strong>INSA Lyon</strong>, carried out
      at the <strong>LIRIS laboratory (CNRS, UMR 5205)</strong> under Professors{' '}
      <strong>Céline Robardet</strong> and <strong>Marc Plantevit</strong>, on machine
      learning for electricity price forecasting on the European day-ahead market. It was a{' '}
      <strong>CIFRE industrial thesis</strong> with <strong>BCM Energy</strong> (then
      trading as Planète Oui, now Elmy), defended on <strong>17 January 2024</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>The research, and why it is here</h2>
    <p>
      A doctorate is worth stating for one reason: it is where the habits came from. Four
      years of being wrong in public, where a bad forecast cost the desk money the same
      afternoon, teaches validation design better than tidy benchmark work does.
    </p>
    <p>
      The thesis, <em>Machine learning techniques for electricity price forecasting</em>,
      predicts the twenty-four hourly prices of a European day-ahead market before they
      are fixed at noon. The literature splits into two incomplete camps.{' '}
      <strong>Expert models</strong> replicate the price-fixing algorithm from estimated
      inputs: theoretically right, inaccurate in practice, because input errors propagate
      through a clearing process never designed to be robust to them.{' '}
      <strong>Data-driven models</strong> estimate prices directly: accurate, and opaque.
      Opacity is not an abstract complaint when the output goes to a trading desk — a trader
      who cannot see why a forecast moved will not size a position on it.{' '}
      <strong>Explainability is an adoption requirement, not a compliance nicety</strong>:
      the most portable lesson in the thesis.
    </p>
    <p class="pub-links">
      <a href="/files/thesis.pdf"><strong>Read the thesis (PDF)</strong></a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/files/defence.pdf">defence slides</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="https://www.youtube.com/live/Stj9HCPnH7g?t=226" target="_blank" rel="noopener">defence video</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Putting an optimisation problem inside a model</h2>
    <p>
      European day-ahead prices are not observed, they are computed: a single algorithm —{' '}
      <strong>EUPHEMIA</strong> — clears every submitted order at once, maximising social
      welfare subject to energy balance and cross-border capacity. Almost every forecasting
      model in the literature treats the market as a black box instead. My DSAA 2023 paper
      does the opposite: it predicts the <strong>order book</strong> from fundamental
      variables, then feeds those orders into a differentiable formulation of the clearing
      problem, so the mechanism is present during training rather than bolted on at the end.
      That required a <strong>differentiable dichotomy search</strong> solving a
      mixed-integer quadratic program inside a neural network.
    </p>
    <p>
      That shape is not confined to electricity. Whenever the quantity you are forecasting
      is itself the output of a known process — a pricing rule, an allocation, an auction, a
      scheduler — the same three options apply: ignore it, replicate it and inherit its
      input sensitivity, or make it differentiable and train through it.
    </p>

